1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
The TCP header is twenty bytes of fixed fields, and a data offset field says how long it is.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Four bits hold 5 to 15 words, so the header is 20 to 60 bytes long.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Sixty minus twenty is forty: every option in the connection shares one budget of 40 bytes.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
MSS, window scale, SACK-permitted and timestamps all want bytes from that same forty.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
MSS is kind 2 and four bytes; window scale is kind 3 and three bytes.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
SACK-permitted is kind 4 and two bytes; timestamps is kind 8 and ten bytes.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Four plus three plus two plus ten is nineteen bytes of option data.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The header must end on a four-byte boundary, so nineteen becomes twenty and the header is forty bytes.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The MSS option says how much TCP data its sender can receive in one segment.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
It counts data only: not the TCP header, not the IP header.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
So MSS = MTU minus 20 for the IPv4 header minus 20 for the TCP header, which is MTU minus 40.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
576 gives 536, 1500 gives 1460, 9000 gives 8960, and IPv6's forty-byte header gives 1440.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
The rule: the MSS is the effective MTU minus only the fixed IP and TCP headers.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Options are not subtracted from the advertised MSS, so 1460 stays 1460.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
But the sender must cut the data by the options it actually sends: 1460 becomes 1440 with 20 bytes of options.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Sending the full 1460 with those options makes a 1520-byte datagram, 20 bytes past a 1500-byte MTU.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The four handshake options spend 19 of the 40 bytes, so 21 remain.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
A TCP MD5 option is 18 bytes: 19 plus 18 is 37, which pads to 40.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
That leaves a 60-byte header and 1420 bytes of data on a 1500-byte MTU.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
One more four-byte option would make 41, so something has to be dropped.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
With a shift count s, the window field counts steps of 2^s bytes.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
So the right edge of the window can only land on ACK plus a whole number of 2^s steps.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
With s = 7 the step is 128 bytes: from ACK 1000 the edges are 1128, 1256 and 1384.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The receiver's buffer ends at 1300, which is not one of those points.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The true window is 1300 bytes from ACK 1000; the field can only say 2, which means 1256.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
After 40 more bytes, ACK 1040 and window 2 give 1296, four bytes short.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Then 5 more bytes: ACK 1045. A field of 2 gives 1301, one byte past the buffer.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
A field of 1 gives 1173, which is 127 bytes short. The advertised window has shrunk.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Receiver rule: honour any segment that would have been in window for any ACK you sent.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Sender rule: the first retransmission may ignore the window if it is out by less than 2^s.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Later retransmissions must lie inside the latest advertised window.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The receiver should also track the largest right edge it has ever advertised.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The worst retraction is 2^s minus one bytes: 127 at a shift count of 7.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
At a shift count of 14 the step is 16,384 bytes, so the worst retraction is 16,383 bytes.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
That is more than eleven 1460-byte segments, so a large step makes a large shrink.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Stacks reduce it by advertising whole segments and by suppressing small window updates.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Budget: forty bytes, the sum of the option lengths rounded up to a multiple of four.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
MSS: the MTU minus the fixed headers, counting data only: 1460 on Ethernet, 1440 over IPv6.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Options do not lower the MSS; they lower the data actually sent.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Scaling can retract a window by up to 2^s minus one bytes, and the rules say to tolerate it.

